# status-caddy.example — a Caddy vhost that serves the static status page.
#
# status-render.sh writes index.html + status.json into
# RUN_ISSUES_STATUS_OUT_DIR (default:
# ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/run-issues/www). This package writes
# ONLY those files. Serving them is the machine owner's decision, so this is an
# example you copy and adapt — nothing here is installed or activated.
#
# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ READ THIS BEFORE EXPOSING THE PAGE                                       │
# │                                                                         │
# │ The page carries repo slugs, issue numbers, PR URLs and branch names.   │
# │ Branch names and repo slugs routinely reveal CLIENT NAMES and internal  │
# │ project structure. There is NO authentication inside the page — access  │
# │ control is a network-layer concern, and it is your job, not this        │
# │ package's.                                                              │
# │                                                                         │
# │ DO NOT put this vhost behind a public tunnel (cloudflared, ngrok, a     │
# │ public reverse proxy) UNLESS it sits behind authenticated access        │
# │ control (e.g. Cloudflare Access, an authenticating proxy, mTLS). A      │
# │ public tunnel with no auth makes every repo/branch/PR world-readable.   │
# │                                                                         │
# │ The safe default below binds to a Tailscale address, so the page is     │
# │ reachable only from your own tailnet and never from the open internet.  │
# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

# Replace 100.x.y.z with THIS machine's Tailscale IP (`tailscale ip -4`), and
# the path with your actual RUN_ISSUES_STATUS_OUT_DIR. `bind` is what keeps the
# listener off every other interface — without it Caddy would listen on 0.0.0.0.
http://100.x.y.z:8080 {
	bind 100.x.y.z
	root * /Users/CHANGE-ME/.local/state/run-issues/www
	file_server {
		index index.html
	}
	# Read-only: refuse anything but GET/HEAD so the static tree cannot be
	# written to over HTTP even by accident.
	@write not method GET HEAD
	respond @write 405
}

# ── If you must expose it beyond the tailnet ────────────────────────────────
# Put an authenticating layer IN FRONT and keep the origin bound to localhost
# or the tailnet. Example shape with Cloudflare Access terminating auth and a
# tunnel pointing at a LOCAL-ONLY origin (never bind the origin to 0.0.0.0):
#
#   http://127.0.0.1:8080 {
#   	root * /Users/CHANGE-ME/.local/state/run-issues/www
#   	file_server { index index.html }
#   }
#
# …with `cloudflared` routing an Access-protected hostname to 127.0.0.1:8080.
# The auth MUST be enforced before the request reaches Caddy — an unauthenticated
# public tunnel to this origin leaks client names. If in doubt, keep it on the
# tailnet.
